["Why does Lucien call himself 'de Rubempré'?","Born Lucien Chardon, son of a pharmacist, he takes his mother's noble name and spends the novel chasing a royal decree to make it legal."],
["What is the setting in Angoulême?","Angoulême on the Charente, where the snobbish upper town despises the commercial suburb of L'Houmeau, where Lucien was born."],
["Who is Madame de Bargeton?","Louise de Bargeton, a provincial bluestocking who takes Lucien to Paris and drops him the moment she sees him in an unfashionable suit."],
["What does Balzac expose about journalism?","Reviews bought and sold, books praised or slaughtered for bribes, editors blackmailing theatres, and actresses paying for good notices, all under the Restoration."],
["What is a claque?","A team of paid applauders hired to cheer a play or an actress; Balzac's character Braulard runs one."],
["What is the Cénacle?","A circle of poor, high-minded young men led by the writer Daniel d'Arthez who live for honest work, the moral counterweight to the newspapers."],
["How does Lucien ruin his brother-in-law?","He forges David Séchard's signature on bills of exchange for 3,000 francs, which the rival Cointet brothers use to have David jailed for debt."],
["What was David's invention?","A cheap way of making paper from vegetable fibres instead of rags; the Cointets take over the process and grow rich from it."],
["Who is Coralie?","The young actress who loves and keeps Lucien in Paris and dies poor after his feuds wreck her career."],
["How does the novel end?","Lucien, about to drown himself, meets a Spanish priest, Carlos Herrera, really the escaped convict Vautrin, who offers him money and success in exchange for obedience."],
["What happens to Lucien in the sequel?","In Splendours and Miseries of Courtesans, Lucien rises under Vautrin's control, is arrested, and hangs himself in his cell at the Conciergerie."],
["What did Oscar Wilde say about Lucien?","In The Decay of Lying Wilde wrote that one of the greatest tragedies of his life was the death of Lucien de Rubempré."],
["Has it been filmed?","Xavier Giannoli's Illusions perdues (2021), with Benjamin Voisin as Lucien, won seven César awards including Best Film."]
