"the-plague":[
["Is Oran a real city?","Yes, Oran is a major Algerian port where Camus lived in 1941-42; it suffered a deadly cholera epidemic in 1849, but the novel's plague is invented."],
["What is the plague an allegory for?","Most readers take it as the German occupation of France, with the volunteer sanitary squads as the Resistance; Camus himself edited the Resistance paper Combat."],
["Who is the narrator?","At the end he reveals himself as Dr Bernard Rieux, who told the story in the third person to bear objective witness."],
["How does the epidemic begin?","On 16 April Rieux finds a dead rat on his landing, and within days thousands of rats are dying in the streets before people start falling ill."],
["What is Joseph Grand's sentence?","The clerk Grand spends years polishing the first sentence of his novel, about an elegant horsewoman riding a sorrel mare through the Bois de Boulogne one May morning."],
["Why does Tarrou fight the plague?","Tarrou, a prosecutor's son, turned against all killing after watching his father demand a death sentence; he organizes the squads and dies of plague just as it ends."],
["How do Father Paneloux's sermons change?","He first preaches that the plague is God's punishment, then after watching magistrate Othon's little son die in agony, preaches that one must believe all or nothing, and soon dies himself."],
["Does Rambert escape?","The Paris journalist Rambert arranges an escape to his lover, but when the chance comes he chooses to stay and work with the squads."],
["What happens to Cottard?","Cottard, a criminal who welcomed the plague because it postponed his arrest, fires on the street from his window when it ends and is dragged off by police."],
["How does The Plague end?","Amid the celebrations Rieux reflects that the plague bacillus never dies but lies dormant in furniture and linen, waiting to send its rats to die in a happy city again."],
["Did Covid revive the book?","Yes: during the 2020 lockdowns its sales soared in Italy, France and Britain, and Penguin had to reprint to meet demand."],
["When was it published and what prizes did it win?","Gallimard published it in 1947, it won the Prix des Critiques, and it helped earn Camus the 1957 Nobel Prize at age 44."],
["How did Camus die?","He was killed in January 1960 near Villeblevin when the car driven by his publisher Michel Gallimard hit a tree; an unused train ticket was in his pocket."]
],
